  (:adr/body "# ADR 2799007122: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7122 Floor Layers and Tile Setters job-site scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7122\n-- Floor Layers and Tile Setters** only, Wave3 (production/trades) of\nthe rollout (Wave0/Wave1/Wave2 100% complete; Wave3 batch1 of 6 --\n7111/7112/7113/7114/7119/7121 -- already landed).\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7122\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"7122\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Floor Layers and Tile Setters\" --\nconfirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's\n`:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies [:robotics\n:identity :audit-ledger]`, `:optional-technologies []` and\n`:operating-states [:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit]`, and\nno `:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7122` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before\nscaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the\n`7122` entry to `:implemented`.\n\n**This actor carries a construction-site physical-safety dimension:**\nfloor layers and tile setters perform interior finishing work on\nactive job sites, with real physical-safety stakes -- kneeling-strain\ninjury, adhesive/solvent fume exposure, and cut hazards from\ntile-cutting tools. The controlling constraint for this vertical is\nthat the closed op allowlist must NEVER include any op that directly\nfinalizes a flooring/tile-installation-execution decision, or\noverrides a site safety officer's judgment -- those decisions must\nalways be a hard permanent block or an always-escalate op, never\nauto-commit-eligible. This actor is deliberately scoped to JOB-SITE\nSCHEDULING/LOGISTICS COORDINATION ONLY -- it never performs\nflooring/tile-installation work itself.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored closely for module SHAPE and the\nclosed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7111`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7111)\n(House Builders, same wave/domain shape, just landed: `store`/\n`advisor`/`governor`/`actor` split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full\ndoc set: README, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md, CONTRIBUTING.md, GOVERNANCE.md,\nLICENSE (AGPL-3.0-or-later), SECURITY.md, docs/business-model.md,\ndocs/operator-guide.md, blueprint.edn) -- cloned and read in full in\nthis session and independently re-verified green before this repo was\nscaffolded.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7122` as an OSS job-site\nscheduling/logistics-coordination actor for floor layers and tile\nsetters, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as every prior\n`cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Floor & Tile Advisor\n⊣ FloorTileGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the\n`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with\nan independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger. All source is\nportable `.cljc` (no JVM interop); tests are `clojure.test` `.clj`,\nmatching the reference repo's own layout.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **job-site scheduling/logistics coordination\nONLY, never direct flooring/tile-installation-execution authority** --\nit must NEVER be designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose\nexercising a flooring/tile-installation-execution decision, or any\noverride of a site safety officer's judgment. These are not merely\ngated behind escalation -- they are structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- task/materials-usage/progress data logging\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a fume-exposure/cut-hazard/site-\n  condition concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER auto-commit eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- flooring/tile-materials procurement\n  proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles** a\nflooring/tile-installation-execution decision or a site-safety-\nofficer-judgment override. These are **structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind escalation -- an op\nsuch as `:finalize-tile-installation-decision` cannot even be proposed\nby the reference mock advisor (its `infer`/`rationale-for` functions\nonly ever emit one of the four allowlist ops), and if any code path (a\nforged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever\nproduced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check\n(`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently. A second,\nindependent HARD check (`:scope-excluded-action`) names five\nconcretely-forbidden ops (`floortile.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:finalize-flooring-installation-decision`,\n`:finalize-tile-installation-decision`,\n`:proceed-with-tile-installation`,\n`:proceed-with-flooring-installation`,\n`:override-site-safety-officer-judgment`) as defense-in-depth, and is\nverified by dedicated unit tests\n(`floortile.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-flooring-installation-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-tile-installation-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-tile-installation`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-site-safety-officer-judgment`).\n\n### Four HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Installer unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered installer (floor-layer/tile-setter crew\n   member) record (`:no-installer`). Never trusts the proposal's own\n   claim of who the installer is.\n2. **Site unregistered** -- any proposal must cite an independently\n   registered job site (`:no-site`). Together with check 1, this\n   directly implements the CRITICAL invariant that a worker/site\n   record must be independently verified/registered before any\n   action.\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance)\n   (`:no-actuation`).\n4. **Op not allowed / scope-excluded action** -- any `:op` outside the\n   closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`),\n   and independently, any proposal whose `:op` names one of the five\n   concretely-forbidden ops above, OR whose free-text `:rationale`\n   names a finalization/execution action for proceeding with tile or\n   flooring installation, or overriding a site safety officer's\n   judgment, is a second HARD, PERMANENT block\n   (`:scope-excluded-action`), evaluated unconditionally on content --\n   defense-in-depth on top of the allowlist check: even if a future\n   change somehow widened the allowlist, content-based rejection still\n   independently blocks flooring/tile-installation-execution/safety-\n   override language wherever it appears in free text.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's implicit always-risky check). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface a fume-exposure/cut-\n  hazard/site-condition observation -- it never resolves the concern\n  itself, and the robot's role ends at \"here is the hazard data,\"\n  never \"here is what I recommend doing about site safety.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a\n  hard block, per the task's explicit domain design: an over-threshold\n  flooring/tile-materials order is not itself unsafe, unlike a\n  flooring/tile-installation-execution or safety-officer-override\n  attempt, which is why it is handled by a different mechanism (soft\n  escalate vs. structural absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"tile\", \"flooring\", \"safety\") can accidentally match inside the mock\nadvisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly the risk\nthis vertical's own vocabulary (a job-site coordination actor working\nadjacent to tile/flooring installation necessarily discusses those\nconcepts to do its scheduling/logging job) could trigger if phrased\ncarelessly. `floortile.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased\nexclusively as finalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g. \"proceed with the\ntile installation\", \"authorize the flooring installation\", \"override\nthe site safety officer's judgment\" -- matching the task's own worked\nexample phrasing), never bare nouns, and\n`floortile.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor, never trips\n`:scope-excluded-action`. The mock advisor's own default rationale\ntemplate was deliberately written to contain the bare nouns (\"logged\nwork record for installer ...\", \"scheduled crew operation for tile-\nsetting task ...\", \"... routed for site safety officer review\")\nWITHOUT containing any of the full finalization-action phrases, so the\nregression test is a genuine stress test of the bare-noun-vs-action-\nphrase distinction, not a vacuous pass. Green on first run in this\nsession -- the scope-exclusion self-trip guard itself was never\ntripped during development. `floortile.actor-test/holds-a-scope-\nexcluded-op-even-at-high-confidence` additionally exercises the same\ninvariant through the full graph run, confirming a scope-excluded\nproposal never reaches `commit-record!` regardless of disposition\npath.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7122\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7122` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7122`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit, to account for concurrent sibling landings in this\n  same batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 21 tests, 45 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-tile-installation-completion`\n  that is merely gated behind an always-escalate list (the shape\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` uses for `:approve-over-ceiling-posting`):\n  rejected outright per the task's explicit domain-design guidance --\n  an always-escalate op still means the *capability* to finalize a\n  flooring/tile-installation-execution decision exists in the code\n  path and is merely deferred to a human gate. For a decision that can\n  affect worker safety (kneeling-strain injury, adhesive/solvent fume\n  exposure, cut hazards from tile-cutting tools) and installation\n  quality, the capability itself must not exist. The only op related\n  to site-safety escalation is `:flag-safety-concern`, which surfaces\n  the need for human/site-safety-officer attention and never itself\n  decides, authorizes, or defers a flooring/tile-installation decision\n  for later automatic execution.\n- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block (mirroring the 3313\n  reference's transaction-amount-ceiling HARD check): rejected per the\n  task's explicit domain design -- an over-threshold flooring/tile-\n  materials order is not itself unsafe, so it is handled as a soft\n  escalate gate, not a hard block; only the flooring/tile-installation-\n  execution/safety-officer-override class is a hard, unconditional\n  block.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"tile\" \"flooring\"\n  \"safety\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class described above (and CLAUDE.md's own worked example for this\n  exact task), and per the explicit instruction to phrase scope-\n  exclusion terms as finalization/execution ACTIONS, never bare nouns\n  -- this actor's own domain necessarily discusses tile/flooring/\n  safety-officer concepts to do its scheduling/logging job, so a\n  bare-noun list would risk self-blocking the actor's own legitimate\n  proposals (regression-tested directly against rationale text that\n  deliberately contains those bare nouns).\n- Merging `installer` and `site` into a single entity (only checking\n  one provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity provenance shape\n  (independently-registered installer AND independently-registered\n  site) matches the CRITICAL invariant that a \"worker/site record must\n  be independently verified/registered before any action\" and the\n  established pattern from prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actors with\n  two-sided provenance checks.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-work-record`** -- task/materials-usage/progress data\n   logging only. Requires an independently registered installer AND\n   site (`:no-installer`/`:no-site`). Not a flooring/tile-\n   installation-execution or safety-officer decision in any respect\n   -- it is a records-management action.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n   only. Cannot carry a flooring/tile-installation-execution decision\n   (structurally absent from the op's field shape; any such attempt\n   via free text is caught by the `:scope-excluded-action` content\n   check). Does not authorize, finalize, or draw any conclusion about\n   a specific flooring or tile installation step -- it is a\n   calendar/logistics action.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces a fume-exposure/cut-hazard/\n   site-condition concern to a human/site safety officer; ALWAYS\n   escalates, unconditionally, at every confidence level, NEVER\n   auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY channel through which a\n   safety observation may be surfaced, and it explicitly never\n   resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself -- the site safety\n   officer reviews and acts entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- flooring/tile-materials\n   procurement proposal only (item, cost). Not a flooring/tile-\n   installation-execution or authorization decision of any kind -- it\n   is a purchasing-logistics action, gated by ordinary cost-ceiling\n   escalation (above 2000 always escalates).\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na flooring/tile-installation-execution decision or a site-safety-\nofficer-judgment override -- these categories are structurally absent,\nnot gated, confirmed by: (a) the closed four-op allowlist itself\n(`floortile.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) five concretely-named\nforbidden-op fixtures rejected at the governor-unit level\n(`floortile.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`floortile.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-flooring-installation-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-tile-installation-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-tile-installation`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-site-safety-officer-judgment`),\nand (c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`\nfield (`floortile.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`,\n`.../contains-excluded-phrase?`), tested directly against a rationale\nthat attempts to proceed with tile installation or override a site\nsafety officer's judgment even via an otherwise-allowlisted op\n(`floortile.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-even-with-allowlisted-op`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-override-site-safety-officer`).\n")))
